Do not remove existing stages, counters, lifecycle correlation, cleanup behavior, or display information.

==================================================
2. PROBLEM TO FIX
==================================================

Today the Producer Monitor is effectively reconstructed from a limited log tail.

For example, the UI can show:

Canonical events in scan window: 0
Scan window: 20,000,000 bytes

and then each producer shows:

No canonical events

This causes previously visible producer information to disappear after it falls outside the current log tail.

The Producer Monitor must no longer depend on the current log tail as its authoritative source of operational state.

==================================================
3. NEW ARCHITECTURE
==================================================

Mutate the Producer Monitor from log-tail reconstruction into persistent Producer Monitor state.

Use a state-file persistence approach similar to the existing bot state-file mechanism.

Do NOT store this inside the trading state itself.

Create a separate BOT OPS Producer Monitor state file so observability cannot interfere with trading state.

Conceptually:

Producer lifecycle event
    ->
update Producer Monitor state
    ->
persist Producer Monitor state
    ->
BOT OPS backend reads state
    ->
existing Producer Monitor cards are derived and displayed

Raw logs remain available for forensic debugging but are no longer the primary source of truth for the Producer Monitor.

Reuse the existing state-file safety mechanism where practical, including synchronization and atomic write/replace behavior.

==================================================
4. DECISION ID IS THE ATTEMPT CORRELATION KEY
==================================================

A producer decision already has a DecisionID created at decision creation time.

DecisionID format is conceptually:

Producer + formatted timestamp to millisecond precision

Example:

Case13APeakSell_20260807T214512.347

All lifecycle events belonging to the same producer attempt must retain the same DecisionID.

Examples:

DecisionID=X stage=produced
DecisionID=X stage=pending_accepted
DecisionID=X stage=filled
DecisionID=X stage=committed

or:

DecisionID=Y stage=produced
DecisionID=Y stage=entry_failed

The DecisionID is the primary correlation key for one producer attempt.

Do not correlate different attempts merely by producer + stage.

==================================================
5. STATE MODEL
==================================================

Use a model equivalent to:

map[EntryProducer]map[string]*ProducerAttempt

where the second map key is DecisionID.

Example:

producerHistory[Case13APeakSell][decisionID]

A ProducerAttempt should contain at least:

type ProducerAttempt struct {
    DecisionID string
    Producer   EntryProducer
    Side       string
    CreatedAt  time.Time

    Events map[ProducerStage]ProducerEvent
}

Use the existing canonical stage type if one already exists. Do not introduce duplicate stage enums unnecessarily.

CreatedAt must represent the original decision creation time.

Although the DecisionID contains a timestamp, keep CreatedAt explicitly as time.Time and use CreatedAt for retention/expiry logic. Do not repeatedly parse time out of DecisionID.

==================================================
6. PRODUCER EVENT MODEL
==================================================

Persist the factual canonical event data required to reconstruct the existing cards and answer the new questions.

A model equivalent to this is appropriate:

type ProducerEvent struct {
    Time     time.Time
    Producer EntryProducer
    Side     string
    Stage    ProducerStage

    DecisionID string
    OrderID    string

    Reason string

    ErrorCode       EntryProduceErrorCode
    Error           string
    CleanupRequired bool

    PnL float64
}

Adapt field types/names to existing code instead of duplicating existing types.

Only persist factual event data.

Do not persist derived card counters as the authoritative source.

==================================================
7. RETENTION MODEL
==================================================

Producer attempts are retained for a rolling 24-hour operational window.

The retention unit is the whole ProducerAttempt / DecisionID.

If:

attempt.CreatedAt < now - 24h

remove the entire attempt.

Do NOT expire individual stage events independently.

All stages of one DecisionID must disappear together so lifecycle correlation is never broken.

Example:

produced
pending_accepted
entry_failed

must remain together until the attempt itself ages beyond 24 hours.

Conceptually:

cutoff := time.Now().Add(-24 * time.Hour)

for producer, attempts := range producerHistory {
    for decisionID, attempt := range attempts {
        if attempt.CreatedAt.Before(cutoff) {
            delete(attempts, decisionID)
        }
    }

    if len(attempts) == 0 {
        delete(producerHistory, producer)
    }
}

Perform cleanup safely and periodically and/or during state mutation.

